From ef2f2ab125c2720f556653b04a41f51db45df774 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rodney Pantonial <rodneypantonial@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 04:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Inventory cleanup: sleep by time used, also inside large
 inventories

The nightly run counted inventories (a sleep every 25), so one inventory with hundreds of items, or 25 large ones, still ran in one frame: every item reads its catalog name. CheckInventories now takes the milliseconds a frame may use (Overwatch passes 10) and sleeps between items as well as between inventories.

Same results as the one-frame check: with a timer, the stacks and items of an inventory are copied first; after a sleep a stack the game removed or replaced is skipped, an item is looked up again (skipped if the game took it out), an empty stack is only removed if the game did not refill it, and objects destroyed meanwhile are skipped (their inventory went with them). Items the game adds while we sleep are left for the next night, as the one-frame check would have run before they came. When a corrupt jam jar's own removal empties its stack, the check of that inventory stops like the dictionary loop does without a timer. A world shutting down ends the run, and ResetException is passed on so the task can stop. Without a timer (0, the menu action and the other mods' callers) the check is unchanged.

Assisted with AI
